from itertools import product
from edtrace import text
from lesson_demos import (STORIES, story, outcome_demo, budget_demo, vote_demo,
                          selection_demo, horizon_demo)


def main():

    text('## 01 · Agent Evaluation and Benchmarks')
    text('CSE 398/498 · Vibe Research for Scientific Discovery · Lichao Sun · Lehigh University')
    text('We have talked about how language models are built, trained, and evaluated. We have also talked about how to build an agent. Today we will look at **how to evaluate agents systematically**.')
    text('AI is moving fast. Systems from companies such as OpenAI and Anthropic can handle increasingly difficult tasks. That makes evaluation a harder research problem: how do we find out what these systems can actually do, and where they still fail?')
    text('**Evaluation itself can become a bottleneck.** Existing tests may be too easy, too narrow, or too different from the work we want an agent to do.')
    text('For ambitious goals such as solving major open problems in mathematics or science, a convincing answer is not enough. We need ways to check correctness and the steps that led to it. This is an evaluation challenge, not evidence that a model has already reached that level.')
    text('This lecture follows that challenge: how earlier work evaluated agents, what different benchmarks measure, and how to design a comparison we can trust.')

    text('## 02 · Today’s Plan')
    text('Start with one task')
    text('- Decide what success looks like. Check the result. Then repeat the run.')
    text('Compare two systems')
    text('- Use the same tasks and account for cost. Try simple methods before adding more steps.')
    text('Read rankings and check graders')
    text('- See how user votes become a ranking. Then see how models grade answers and actions.')
    text('Choose the right test')
    text('- Compare expert questions, software work, and office tasks. Ask which one matches the work you want an agent to do.')

    text('## 03 · What Is Task Success?')
    text('**Task success needs a clear definition before we compare agents.** The definition depends on the requested outcome and the rules of the environment.')
    text('- Specify the outcome the agent must achieve.')
    text('- Include constraints, such as policies and limits on permitted actions.')
    text('- Choose evidence that can establish completion: environment state, tests, or a checked answer.')
    text("An agent's final message and its action sequence provide evidence, but neither alone guarantees that the task was completed.")
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1: τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment">[1]</a> Course synthesis grounded in tau squared benchmark task evaluation, ICML 2026</div>')

    text('## 04 · *τ²-Bench*')
    text('To evaluate agents systematically, a benchmark needs tasks, policies, tools, and repeatable runs. **τ²-Bench studies customer support**, including tasks that require coordination between an agent and a simulated customer.')
    text('- The agent reads a request and uses tools to check or change records.')
    text('- Retail tasks involve orders and returns. Airline tasks involve bookings.')
    text('- Phone support adds another difficulty. The customer can change phone settings while the agent uses support tools.')
    text('For example, the agent may ask the customer to turn off airplane mode and report whether service returns. Both sides act, so the conversation affects the result.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_04_02.png"><img src="images/w12_04_02.png" alt="τ²-Bench · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1: τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment">[1]</a> Barres et al. · ICML 2026 · PMLR 306, pp. 6736–6782</div></figure>')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_tau_setup.png"><img src="images/w12_tau_setup.png" alt="The support agent and simulated customer talk to each other and use their own tools."><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><figcaption class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1">[1]</a> Figure 1 from the authors’ <a href="https://arxiv.org/html/2506.07982v1#S1.F1" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">τ²-Bench preprint</a> · CC BY 4.0</figcaption></figure>')
    text('In the setup figure, follow the conversation between the agent and customer. Then follow each side to its tools. **The agent must find a fix and help the customer carry it out.**')
    text('Next, we will look at one airline task to see how the benchmark decides whether a run passed.')

    text('## 05 · When an Agent Should Say No')
    text('This real airline task from the *τ²-Bench* repository asks for a cancellation that the policy does not allow. The expected behavior is to refuse the request and explain why.')
    text('**Success is defined by the task and its policy.** The original record and JSON excerpt show how the benchmark records the expected refusal.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_05_02.png"><img src="images/w12_05_02.png" alt="Success Can Mean Refusing an Invalid Action · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-2" aria-label="Reference 2: τ²-Bench Airline Tasks">[2]</a> Sierra Research · Benchmark repository</div></figure><article id="ref-2" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_05_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[2] τ²-Bench Airline Tasks</h3><p class="reference-meta">Sierra Research · Benchmark repository</p><p>Task records and success criteria for the airline domain.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://github.com/sierra-research/tau2-bench/blob/main/data/tau2/domains/airline/tasks.json" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('<div class="source-code"><strong>Airline task 0 · JSON excerpt</strong><pre><code>{\n  &quot;id&quot;: &quot;0&quot;,\n  &quot;evaluation_criteria&quot;: {\n    &quot;actions&quot;: [],\n    &quot;communicate_info&quot;: [],\n    &quot;nl_assertions&quot;: [\n      &quot;Agent should refuse to proceed with the cancellation.&quot;\n    ],\n    &quot;reward_basis&quot;: [\n      &quot;DB&quot;,\n      &quot;COMMUNICATE&quot;\n    ]\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>')
    text('- DB refers to checks of the database state. COMMUNICATE refers to required information given to the user.')
    text('- nl_assertions lists checks written in plain language. Here it names the expected refusal.')
    text('**An allowed result is part of task success.** Once we can grade one run, we can ask whether the agent gets it right again.')

    text('## 06 · One Win or Every Run?')
    text('The same agent can produce different outcomes on repeated runs. *τ²-Bench* therefore measures success across multiple attempts of each task.')
    text('Two metrics answer different questions about those attempts.')
    text('- **pass@k** asks whether at least one of k attempts succeeds. This matters when we can try again and identify a successful result.')
    text('- **pass^k** asks whether all k attempts succeed. This matters when every customer needs the right result on their own attempt.')
    text('At k = 1, both score one attempt. As k grows, the two questions become quite different.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1: τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment">[1]</a> τ²-Bench, ICML 2026 (evaluation metrics)</div>')
    text('The calculation below is a teaching example. Each run has a 50% chance of success, and the runs are independent. The 16 four-run outcomes are equally likely.')
    text('Use the buttons to count the outcomes that satisfy each rule.')
    outcomes = list(product((False, True), repeat=4))
    any_rate = sum(map(any, outcomes)) / len(outcomes)  # @inspect any_rate
    all_rate = sum(map(all, outcomes)) / len(outcomes)  # @clear any_rate @inspect all_rate
    text(outcome_demo(outcomes, any_rate, all_rate))  # @clear all_rate
    text("These are teaching numbers from a simple probability model. Next we will read the benchmark's measured results, where tasks have different levels of difficulty.")

    text('## 07 · Success Across Runs')
    text('The authors repeat tasks and report **pass^k**. Each panel below uses one model. The colors separate retail, airline, and phone support tasks.')
    text('- Start at k = 1. This is success on one attempt.')
    text('- Move toward k = 4. A task now needs four successful runs to count.')
    text('- Compare colors within the same model panel before comparing models.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_07_02.png"><img src="images/w12_07_02.png" alt="τ²-Bench: Reliability Across Repeated Runs · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1: τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment">[1]</a> τ²-Bench, ICML 2026 (Figure 3, original experimental results)</div></figure><article id="ref-1" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_07_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[1] τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment</h3><p class="reference-meta">Barres et al. · ICML 2026 · PMLR 306, pp. 6736–6782</p><p>Repeated success and the effects of user coordination.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v306/barres26a.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('The bars generally fall as k increases. A good single-run score can still include tasks that fail on another attempt.')
    text('Phone support is especially hard in these results. The next experiment asks which part of that task causes trouble.')

    text('## 08 · Who Controls the Tools?')
    text('Phone support requires both choosing a fix and guiding the customer. The authors change the setup to test how these demands affect the score.')
    text('- **Default** keeps the agent and customer on separate sets of tools.')
    text('- **No user** lets the agent use both sets of tools directly.')
    text('- **Oracle plan** gives the agent the correct steps in the controlled setup.')
    text('The first chart uses the original policy. The second adds a workflow to the policy. Read the mode labels along the bottom and compare the two models within each mode.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_08_02.png"><img src="images/w12_08_02.png" alt="τ²-Bench: Reasoning and User Coordination · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_08_03.png"><img src="images/w12_08_03.png" alt="τ²-Bench: Reasoning and User Coordination · image 2"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-1" aria-label="Reference 1: τ²-Bench: Evaluating Conversational Agents in a Dual-Control Environment">[1]</a> τ²-Bench, ICML 2026 (Figure 4, original Telecom ablation)</div></figure>')
    text('Removing the need to guide a user improves both models in the first chart. Giving a plan helps further there. The second chart shows that adding guidance does not improve every condition.')
    text('**A score depends on the whole setup.** We have checked repeated success and changed one part of the task. Next we will compare the cost of different agent designs.')

    text('## 09 · *AI Agents That Matter*')
    text('We have seen how task setup changes success. But a more complex agent may also make more calls and spend more money. **Does its design help, or does it simply get more attempts?** *AI Agents That Matter* studies how to make that comparison fair.')
    text('We will follow two checks from the paper. First compare against cheap, simple methods. Then ask whether the test supports a claim about new tasks.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_09_01.png"><img src="images/w12_09_01.png" alt="AI Agents That Matter · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3: AI Agents That Matter">[3]</a> Kapoor et al. · TMLR 2025</div></figure>')

    text('## 10 · Simple Baselines')
    text('A complicated agent may get more attempts than the system it is compared with. Start by giving a simple method a fair chance.')
    text('- **Retry** asks the model to try the task again.')
    text('- **Warming** raises the sampling temperature to get more varied attempts.')
    text('- **Escalation** tries a stronger model after an earlier attempt fails.')
    text('These methods also use time and money. Record those costs, then compare them with the proposed agent under a similar budget.')
    text('The next figure shows why this changes how we read a coding result.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3: AI Agents That Matter">[3]</a> AI Agents That Matter, TMLR 2025; 2024 preprint (Sections 2.2 and 2.3)</div>')

    text('## 11 · Accuracy and Cost on *HumanEval*')
    text('*HumanEval* asks a model to write short Python functions. Tests check the code. The paper compares methods over 164 problems and five runs.')
    text('- The vertical axis shows accuracy. Higher means more problems solved.')
    text('- The horizontal axis shows cost in US dollars at April 2024 prices. Notice the break in the axis.')
    text('- LDB means Large Language Model Debugger. LATS means Language Agent Tree Search.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_11_01.png"><img src="images/w12_11_01.png" alt="Accuracy and Cost on HumanEval · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3: AI Agents That Matter">[3]</a> AI Agents That Matter, TMLR 2025; 2024 preprint (Figure 1; April 2024 prices; note the broken axes)</div></figure><article id="ref-3" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_11_01.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[3] AI Agents That Matter</h3><p class="reference-meta">Kapoor et al. · TMLR 2025</p><p>Cost, simple baselines, holdout sets, and reliable agent comparisons.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://openreview.net/forum?id=Zy4uFzMviZ" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.01502" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View preprint ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('Look for points near the upper left. Several simple baselines reach similar accuracy to more expensive agent methods in this comparison.')
    text('The small example below uses invented costs to show how a fixed budget can change which system we choose.')
    runs = [('A', 0.70, 2), ('B', 0.80, 9)]  # accuracy and cents per task
    eligible = [(name, score) for name, score, cents in runs if cents <= 5]
    text(budget_demo([(name, score) for name, score, _ in runs], eligible))
    text('Cost tells us what we paid for the measured accuracy. It still does not tell us whether the method works beyond the tasks used to build it. Next we check what was kept separate for testing.')

    text('## 12 · Testing on New Tasks')
    text('An agent may work well on the tasks used to build it. To find out what it can do next, keep some cases separate for testing. These are **held-out cases**.')
    text('- If the claim concerns new instances of a familiar task, hold out fresh instances.')
    text('- If the claim is about new tasks, hold out whole task types.')
    text('- If the claim is about new domains, test a different kind of environment.')
    text("The paper reviews 17 benchmarks. Its table compares the breadth of each benchmark's claim with what it holds out.")
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_12_02.png"><img src="images/w12_12_02.png" alt="Test Sets and Claims About Generalization · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3: AI Agents That Matter">[3]</a> Kapoor et al. · TMLR 2025</div></figure>')
    text('Read the middle column before the counts. It says what needs to be new for each kind of claim. Many surveyed benchmarks lacked that kind of test.')
    text('Now we can ask whether a disappointing result comes from the agent, the task, or the way we ran the test.')
    text(story(STORIES[12][0], *STORIES[12][1]))

    text('## 13 · Why Did the Run Fail?')
    text('Suppose a tool call fails. The agent might have sent the wrong arguments. The service might also have been unavailable. A single failure count hides that difference.')
    text('- Open the run log and find the first incorrect step.')
    text('- Check the task input, tool responses, and final state.')
    text('- Repeat the run to see whether the result changes.')
    text('- When comparing agents, keep the tasks, tool access, and budgets comparable.')
    text('So far we have used outcomes and tests to decide success. Next we will look at tasks where people judge the answer.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-3" aria-label="Reference 3: AI Agents That Matter">[3]</a> AI Agents That Matter, TMLR 2025; 2024 preprint (Section 6); practical synthesis</div>')

    text('## 14 · Votes, Judges, and Outcomes')
    text('**Who decides whether the result is good?** Some tasks have an outcome that can be checked directly. Open responses may require judgment about clarity, usefulness, or correctness.')
    text('- A direct check asks whether the requested state or answer is correct.')
    text('- A human vote asks which answer a person prefers.')
    text('- A model judge reads the task and result, then applies a grading rule.')
    text('We will start with human votes in *Chatbot Arena*, then examine the rankings and the use of model judges.')

    text('## 15 · *Chatbot Arena*')
    text("Direct checks work when the required outcome is clearly specified. Open responses can have several useful versions. **How do we compare them from the user's perspective?** *Chatbot Arena* collects human preferences for this purpose.")
    text('This lets the evaluation include prompts chosen by users. The score reflects the preferences and prompts collected on the platform.')
    text('Before reading the ranking, we need to understand what a vote measures and how it is collected.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_15_01.png"><img src="images/w12_15_01.png" alt="Chatbot Arena · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4: Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference">[4]</a> Chiang et al. · ICML 2024</div></figure>')

    text('## 16 · Right Answers and User Votes')
    text('For a math problem with a known solution, we can check the answer. For a request to explain an idea, users may value clarity, detail, or style.')
    text('- *MMLU*, Massive Multitask Language Understanding, tests knowledge across subjects.')
    text('- *GSM8K* contains grade school math problems. *HellaSwag* tests commonsense completion.')
    text('- *MT Bench* uses conversations with multiple turns. *AlpacaEval* compares responses to instructions.')
    text('The figure places several approaches side by side. They use different tasks and different ways to judge answers.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_16_01.png"><img src="images/w12_16_01.png" alt="Benchmarks for Correctness and Human Preference · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4: Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference">[4]</a> Chiang et al. · ICML 2024</div></figure>')
    text('Fixed tests can become less useful when models have seen the questions or when most scores approach the top. User comparisons offer another view, with their own limits.')

    text('## 17 · Voting in *Chatbot Arena*')
    text('Here is how one Arena comparison works. The same prompt goes to two models. The interface shows both answers before revealing the names.')
    text('- The user can choose A, choose B, or indicate a tie or that both answers are bad.')
    text("- Hiding the names reduces the chance that a vote is based on the model's reputation.")
    text("- A vote still depends on this prompt and this user's judgment.")
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_17_02.png"><img src="images/w12_17_02.png" alt="Chatbot Arena: Voting Rules · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4: Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference">[4]</a> Chiang et al. · ICML 2024</div></figure><article id="ref-4" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_17_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[4] Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference</h3><p class="reference-meta">Chiang et al. · ICML 2024</p><p>Blind pairwise comparisons and preference scores.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://proceedings.mlr.press/v235/chiang24b.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('One vote is a comparison between two answers. The next step combines many comparisons into model scores.')

    text('## 18 · From Votes to Scores')
    text('A model that wins six of ten votes against one opponent has a 60% win share in those votes. A leaderboard has many opponents, so we need to combine the comparisons.')
    text('The **Bradley–Terry model** assigns each model a score. The difference between two scores gives a predicted chance that one wins.')
    text('- Equal scores give a predicted win chance of 50%.')
    text('- A larger score difference gives the higher-scoring model a larger predicted chance of winning.')
    text("- In the equation, ξ labels a model's score. H marks the observed winner of a comparison.")
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_18_01.png"><img src="images/w12_18_01.png" alt="Chatbot Arena: Scores from Pairwise Votes · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4: Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference">[4]</a> Chiang et al. · ICML 2024</div></figure>')
    text('The two-model calculation below illustrates the win share. The full leaderboard fits scores across many pairs, and rankings are less certain when evidence is sparse.')
    text('The Python example uses six wins and four losses with no ties. It computes a 60% win share. A real leaderboard fits scores across many model pairs.')
    wins, losses = 6, 4
    fitted_probability = wins / (wins + losses)  # @inspect fitted_probability
    text(vote_demo(wins, losses, fitted_probability))  # @clear fitted_probability
    text('So far, we have asked how to turn recorded votes into scores. The next paper asks an earlier question: **which model versions get recorded and shown to the public?**')

    text('## 19 · *The Leaderboard Illusion*')
    text('We have seen how votes become scores. Now ask an earlier question: **which model versions reach the public leaderboard?** *The Leaderboard Illusion* studies how private testing and selective reporting can affect the ranking.')
    text('We will first look at which versions become public. Then a simulation will show why picking the best observed score can raise the reported result.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_19_01.png"><img src="images/w12_19_01.png" alt="The Leaderboard Illusion · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-5" aria-label="Reference 5: The Leaderboard Illusion">[5]</a> Singh et al. · NeurIPS 2025 · Datasets and Benchmarks</div></figure>')

    text('## 20 · Which Models Get Reported?')
    text('The diagram shows the selection step. Small dots represent privately tested variants. The large dot represents the version made public.')
    text('- Readers of the public ranking see the selected version.')
    text('- They may not know how many other versions were tried.')
    text('- To assess the comparison, we need testing and removal rules that can be checked.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_20_02.png"><img src="images/w12_20_02.png" alt="Private Testing and Selective Score Reporting · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-5" aria-label="Reference 5: The Leaderboard Illusion">[5]</a> Singh et al. · NeurIPS 2025 · Datasets and Benchmarks</div></figure>')
    text('Selecting a high score is especially misleading when part of the difference comes from chance. The next simulation holds the underlying mean fixed to isolate that effect.')

    text('## 21 · Picking the Highest Score')
    text("In the paper's simulation, the true mean stays at 1200. The experiment increases the number of private variants and reports a selected score.")
    text('- The horizontal axis counts the variants tested.')
    text('- The dashed line marks the unchanged true mean.')
    text('- The rising curve shows how the reported score can increase through selection.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_21_01.png"><img src="images/w12_21_01.png" alt="Selecting the Best Variant Inflates the Score · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-5" aria-label="Reference 5: The Leaderboard Illusion">[5]</a> The Leaderboard Illusion, NeurIPS 2025 (Figure 7; simulation, not a live model ranking)</div></figure><article id="ref-5" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_21_01.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[5] The Leaderboard Illusion</h3><p class="reference-meta">Singh et al. · NeurIPS 2025 · Datasets and Benchmarks</p><p>Private testing and selective reporting of model variants.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/file/70a93f260a51123b3c0e33ecd1b4de97-Paper-Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('More testing creates more chances to pick a high observed score. A fresh test helps check whether the selected model keeps its lead.')
    text("The small Python example below shows the selection operation with invented scores. It uses a different scale from the paper's simulation.")
    scores = [0.68, 0.73, 0.69, 0.76, 0.71]
    selected = max(scores)  # @inspect selected
    text(selection_demo(scores, selected))  # @clear selected
    text('After selecting a version, test it on fresh cases to see whether its lead remains. We can now return to the larger question: what kind of claim does a leaderboard score actually support?')

    text('## 22 · What Does the Score Tell Us?')
    text('We have seen how votes produce a ranking and how reporting choices can affect it. Keep three questions separate when reading any score.')
    text('- Which answer did users prefer? Look at votes from the relevant users and prompts.')
    text('- Did the agent finish the task? Check the required outcome.')
    text('- Will it work on new tasks? Test tasks kept separate from development.')
    text('Collecting human judgments for every new run can be expensive. That leads to the next question. Can a model do some of the grading?')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-4" aria-label="Reference 4: Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference">[4]</a> Chatbot Arena, ICML 2024; AI Agents That Matter, TMLR 2025; course synthesis</div>')

    text('## 23 · LLMs as Judges')
    text('Human comparisons are useful, but checking hundreds of answers after every project update takes time. **Can a model help with the grading?** *Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena* studies this idea and checks where model judges go wrong.')
    text('This can make evaluation cheaper to run, but the grader becomes another component we need to check. First, look at what its prompt asks it to do.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_23_01.png"><img src="images/w12_23_01.png" alt="Judging LLM as a Judge · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-6" aria-label="Reference 6: Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena">[6]</a> Zheng et al. · NeurIPS 2023</div></figure>')

    text('## 24 · A Judge Prompt')
    text('The judge prompt below gives the model a job, criteria, and an output format. These choices help define what its answer means.')
    text('- Find the user question and the two candidate answers.')
    text('- Find the criteria the judge is asked to consider.')
    text('- Find how it must report A, B, or a tie.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_24_02.png"><img src="images/w12_24_02.png" alt="The Default Pairwise Judge Prompt · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-6" aria-label="Reference 6: Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena">[6]</a> Zheng et al. · NeurIPS 2023</div></figure><article id="ref-6" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_24_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[6] Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena</h3><p class="reference-meta">Zheng et al. · NeurIPS 2023</p><p>Pairwise grading prompts and judge bias.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/91f18a1287b398d378ef22505bf41832-Abstract-Datasets_and_Benchmarks.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('A clear prompt makes the grading rule easier to inspect. We still need examples with human judgments to see whether the model follows that rule.')

    text('## 25 · Check Your Judge')
    text('Keep the two answers fixed and swap their positions. If the judge follows the first position instead of the better answer, the order is affecting its decision.')
    text('- Start with cases that people have checked.')
    text('- Swap A and B, then compare the decisions.')
    text('- For a pass/fail grader, count wrongly passed tasks and wrongly failed tasks separately.')
    text('- Save the judge model, prompt, and scoring rule with the results.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-6" aria-label="Reference 6: Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena">[6]</a> Judging LLM as a Judge with MT Bench and Chatbot Arena, NeurIPS 2023; practical synthesis</div>')
    text('Keep the answers fixed and switch their order. The example judge always picks the first answer, exposing the mistake we want to catch.')
    text(story(STORIES[25][0], *STORIES[25][1]))
    text("So far, the judge has compared text. Next we give it evidence of an agent's actions so it can check whether a task was completed.")

    text('## 26 · Grading Digital Agents')
    text('A judge can compare written answers, but an agent may say it finished while leaving the task undone. **What evidence should we give a judge to check an action?** *Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents* studies task completion using evidence such as screenshots.')
    text('We will follow one failed task, look at two grader designs, and then compare the rankings produced by action matching and outcome checks.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_26_01.png"><img src="images/w12_26_01.png" alt="Autonomous Evaluation of Digital Agents · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-7" aria-label="Reference 7: Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents">[7]</a> Pan et al. · COLM 2024</div></figure>')

    text('## 27 · Grading from Screenshots')
    text('In this example, the request is to find who made the most commits to a project. The agent reaches a commits page, but its final response says it cannot determine the answer.')
    text("The grader reads the instruction and the screenshot evidence. Reaching a related page is only an intermediate step. The requested result is a person's name.")
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_27_01.png"><img src="images/w12_27_01.png" alt="Judging Task Completion from Screenshots · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-7" aria-label="Reference 7: Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents">[7]</a> Pan et al. · COLM 2024</div></figure>')
    text("Read the instruction at the top, then the agent's response. The grader marks this run as a failure. Next, we will see two ways to build such a grader.")

    text('## 28 · Two Ways to Read Screenshots')
    text('The diagram starts from the same task and screenshots. It compares two routes to a judgment.')
    text('- A **modular grader** first describes the images in text. A language model then uses that description to grade the task.')
    text('- An **end-to-end grader** uses a model that reads images and text together and makes the judgment directly.')
    text('VLM means vision language model. LM means language model.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_28_02.png"><img src="images/w12_28_02.png" alt="Modular and End to End Evaluators · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-7" aria-label="Reference 7: Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents">[7]</a> Pan et al. · COLM 2024</div></figure><article id="ref-7" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_28_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[7] Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents</h3><p class="reference-meta">Pan et al. · COLM 2024</p><p>Compare action matching with checks of completed tasks.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://openreview.net/forum?id=NPAQ6FKSmK" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a><a href="https://nlp.cs.berkeley.edu/pubs/Pan-Zhang-Tomlin-Zhou-Levine-Suhr_2024_Autonomous_paper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Paper PDF ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('Follow the arrows to the verdict. The modular route lets us inspect the description, but missing details there can affect the final judgment. Both routes need to be checked against human grading.')

    text('## 29 · Matching Actions vs. Finishing')
    text('Another way to grade an app agent is to compare its actions with a recorded human path. But users can reach the same goal by different paths, and a similar path can still stop too early.')
    text('This figure uses *Android in the Wild*, or AitW. Each task asks an agent to follow an instruction in a phone app.')
    text('- The numbers in parentheses are action matching scores.')
    text('- The bars show completed tasks according to human and model graders.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_29_01.png"><img src="images/w12_29_01.png" alt="Action Matching Can Give the Wrong Ranking · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-7" aria-label="Reference 7: Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents">[7]</a> Autonomous Evaluation and Refinement of Digital Agents, COLM 2024 (Figure 2)</div></figure>')
    text('The two measures give different pictures of performance. Read the task outcome before treating a high action matching score as success.')
    text('**Action matching and task completion measure different things.** The paper compares them to show why an evaluation should check the intended outcome. We next examine the kinds of tasks different benchmarks cover.')

    text('## 30 · Differences Between Benchmarks')
    text('**Choose tasks that match the job.** We now know how graders can change a result. Even a careful grader can only tell us about the tasks we give it.')
    text('- HLE tests answers to expert questions.')
    text('- METR tests software tasks of different lengths, measured by human completion time.')
    text('- *TheAgentCompany* tests work across tools in a simulated company.')

    text("## 31 · *Humanity's Last Exam*")
    text("We now turn from grading rules to the tasks being tested. **If many models score near the top, how can we measure further progress?** *Humanity's Last Exam*, or HLE, provides harder questions written by experts.")
    text('The task is to answer the question. Before interpreting the accuracy, we should look at how the questions were selected and reviewed.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_31_01.png"><img src="images/w12_31_01.png" alt="Humanity&#x27;s Last Exam · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-8" aria-label="Reference 8: A benchmark of expert-level academic questions to assess AI capabilities">[8]</a> Center for AI Safety, Scale AI &amp; HLE Contributors Consortium · Nature 649, 1139–1146 (2026)</div></figure>')

    text('## 32 · How HLE Was Built')
    text('Hard questions still need clear wording and correct answers. HLE uses expert contributions and review to build its question set.')
    text('- Experts propose questions and solutions.')
    text('- A model difficulty check filters out questions that the tested models can already answer.')
    text('- Reviewers check whether the answer is correct and the wording is clear.')
    text('- The public benchmark contains 2,500 questions. The project also keeps a private test set.')
    text('The figure shows the process behind the questions. Follow a question through the checks before it reaches the benchmark.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_32_02.png"><img src="images/w12_32_02.png" alt="HLE: Building the Question Set · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-8" aria-label="Reference 8: A benchmark of expert-level academic questions to assess AI capabilities">[8]</a> Center for AI Safety, Scale AI &amp; HLE Contributors Consortium · Nature 649, 1139–1146 (2026)</div></figure><article id="ref-8" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_32_02.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[8] A benchmark of expert-level academic questions to assess AI capabilities</h3><p class="reference-meta">Center for AI Safety, Scale AI &amp; HLE Contributors Consortium · Nature 649, 1139–1146 (2026)</p><p>Humanity&#x27;s Last Exam and its expert question review process.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-025-09962-4" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.14249" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View preprint ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('The work of building the test is part of the evidence behind its score. We still need to describe the settings used when a model takes it.')

    text('## 33 · What HLE Can Tell Us')
    text('An HLE score tells us how often a model answers these questions correctly under the reported settings.')
    text('- State whether the model can browse or run code.')
    text('- State how many attempts it gets and how the final answer is chosen.')
    text('- Check calibration if confidence is reported. Answers given 80% confidence should be right about 80% of the time.')
    text("To assess a longer research or software workflow, run tasks that require those steps. That brings us to METR's software task study.")
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-8" aria-label="Reference 8: A benchmark of expert-level academic questions to assess AI capabilities">[8]</a> Humanity&#x27;s Last Exam, Nature 2026; arXiv 2501.14249 (scope and limitations)</div>')

    text('## 34 · Long Software Tasks')
    text("Answering a hard question does not show that an agent can finish a longer workflow. **How does success change as the work gets longer?** METR's *Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks* uses human task time as a reference.")
    text('The next two figures show how the tasks are grouped and how to read the resulting time horizon.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_34_01.png"><img src="images/w12_34_01.png" alt="Long Software Tasks · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-9" aria-label="Reference 9: Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks">[9]</a> Kwa et al. · NeurIPS 2025</div></figure>')

    text('## 35 · What Is a Time Horizon?')
    text('METR stands for Model Evaluation and Threat Research. Its method combines human timing and agent runs.')
    text('- People complete tasks so the study can estimate their duration.')
    text('- Agents attempt the tasks, and the study records success or failure.')
    text('- A fitted curve relates agent success to human task time.')
    text('The task sets include HCAST, Human Calibrated Autonomy Software Tasks, SWAA, Software Atomic Actions, and *RE Bench*, Research Engineering Benchmark.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_35_01.png"><img src="images/w12_35_01.png" alt="METR: Estimating the Time Horizon · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-9" aria-label="Reference 9: Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks">[9]</a> Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks, NeurIPS 2025 (Figure 2)</div></figure>')
    text('A **50% time horizon** is the human task duration where the fitted agent success rate reaches 50%. It gives us one point on the curve. Next we will locate it in a measured result.')

    text('## 36 · Success by Task Length')
    text("This panel shows Claude 3.7 Sonnet in the paper's setup. The horizontal axis is human completion time. The vertical axis is the agent's chance of success.")
    text('- Start at 50% on the vertical axis and move across to the curve.')
    text('- Move down to read the time. The marked estimate is about 54 minutes.')
    text('- If we require 90% success, the crossing occurs at a shorter task duration.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_36_01.png"><img src="images/w12_36_01.png" alt="METR: Success Probability and Human Task Time · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-9" aria-label="Reference 9: Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks">[9]</a> Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks, NeurIPS 2025 (Figure 4, selected panel)</div></figure><article id="ref-9" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_36_01.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[9] Measuring AI Ability to Complete Long Software Tasks</h3><p class="reference-meta">Kwa et al. · NeurIPS 2025</p><p>Relate task success to the time a human expert needs.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/85069585133c4c168c865e65d72e9775-Abstract-Conference.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('The time measures how long a human needs for the task. The agent may take a different amount of time.')
    text('The Python example below draws an invented curve with a 30-minute crossing. Use it to practice reading the axes. The measured 54-minute result is in the original figure above.')
    minutes = list(range(0, 61, 5))
    probabilities = [1 / (1 + (t / 30) ** 1.4) for t in minutes]
    crossings = [t for t, p in zip(minutes, probabilities) if p <= 0.5]
    horizon = min(crossings)  # @inspect horizon
    text(horizon_demo(minutes, probabilities, horizon))  # @clear horizon
    text('That leads to another evaluation problem: what if the coding task or its test is wrong? The next audit examines the benchmark itself rather than assuming every failed check is an agent failure.')

    text('## 37 · Checking Coding Tasks')
    text("The time horizon depends on which tasks count as passed. **What if the task or test itself is wrong?** OpenAI's *Separating signal from noise in coding evaluations* audits the 731-task public split of *SWE-Bench Pro*.")
    text('The process screens tasks using attempts and failure traces, then reviews flagged cases with agents and people. The figure shows these stages.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_37_01.png"><img src="images/w12_37_01.png" alt="Codex: Auditing Coding Benchmark Tasks · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-10" aria-label="Reference 10: Separating signal from noise in coding evaluations">[10]</a> OpenAI, Separating signal from noise in coding evaluations, Jul 2026 (original methodology figure)</div></figure><article id="ref-10" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_37_01.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[10] Separating signal from noise in coding evaluations</h3><p class="reference-meta">OpenAI · 2026</p><p>Audit the tasks and tests behind software benchmark scores.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://openai.com/index/separating-signal-from-noise-coding-evaluations/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('The agent-assisted review identified 200 broken tasks. The human campaign identified 249. These are two review results, so they should not be added together.')
    text('Examples include hidden requirements, overly strict tests, and tests that miss incomplete fixes. **Sometimes the scoring rule needs repair too.**')

    text('## 38 · What to Save')
    text('We have checked outcomes, costs, graders, and task sets. Save those details together so another person can understand the score.')
    text('- Save the task IDs, inputs, and success checks.')
    text('- Save model and tool versions, budgets, and the baseline.')
    text('- Save every run, its final state, and any tool failures.')
    text('- Save the grader prompt and the cases checked by people.')
    text('A number such as 70% is easier to interpret when we can see what was counted and how it was checked.')
    text('<div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-10" aria-label="Reference 10: Separating signal from noise in coding evaluations">[10]</a> Practical synthesis from the research papers and the OpenAI July 2026 audit</div>')

    text('## 39 · Optional: *TheAgentCompany*')
    text('We have examined success rules, budgets, graders, and task scope separately. **How do they fit together in a complete workflow?** *TheAgentCompany* puts agents in a simulated workplace with documents, chat, websites, and code.')
    text('It checks task completion and can also give credit for intermediate milestones. We will look at the environment first, then the scoring.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_39_01.png"><img src="images/w12_39_01.png" alt="Optional: TheAgentCompany · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-11" aria-label="Reference 11: TheAgentCompany: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Consequential Real World Tasks">[11]</a> Xu et al. · NeurIPS 2025 · Datasets and Benchmarks</div></figure>')

    text('## 40 · Workplace Tasks')
    text('The benchmark contains 175 tasks. The figure shows the workplace tools on the left, tasks on the right, and evaluation below.')
    text('GitLab stores code. ownCloud holds files. RocketChat handles conversations. Plane tracks projects and tasks.')
    text('- Tasks include arranging a meeting, analyzing a spreadsheet, and preparing a code release.')
    text('- The agent can move between tools and interact with simulated colleagues.')
    text('- The evaluator checks the required outcome and specified milestones.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_40_01.png"><img src="images/w12_40_01.png" alt="TheAgentCompany: Tasks Across Workplace Tools · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-11" aria-label="Reference 11: TheAgentCompany: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Consequential Real World Tasks">[11]</a> Xu et al. | NeurIPS 2025 | Figure 1 | Original benchmark design</div></figure><article id="ref-11" class="reference-card"><img src="images/w12_40_01.png" alt="" loading="lazy"><div><h3>[11] TheAgentCompany: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Consequential Real World Tasks</h3><p class="reference-meta">Xu et al. · NeurIPS 2025 · Datasets and Benchmarks</p><p>Workplace tasks that span tools and score intermediate milestones.</p><div class="reference-links"><a href="https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/file/0d744742f6fac4d1134c019b7cef3c8a-Paper-Datasets_and_Benchmarks_Track.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open source ↗</a></div></div></article>')
    text('For a task with several milestones, finishing an early step is useful progress. It still leaves the question of whether the full request was completed.')

    text('## 41 · Scores by Task and Tool')
    text('The result figure groups scores by platform and by task type. That helps us see which kinds of work are easier or harder for the tested systems.')
    text('- SDE means software development engineering. PM means project management. DS means data science.')
    text('- Admin means administration. HR means human resources.')
    text('- Full success requires the complete task. Partial credit includes specified intermediate progress.')
    text('<figure class="lesson-figure"><div class="figure-strip"><button type="button" class="figure-thumb" data-figure="images/w12_41_01.png"><img src="images/w12_41_01.png" alt="TheAgentCompany: Results by Platform and Task · image 1"><span>Enlarge figure ↗</span></button></div><div class="lesson-cite"><a href="#ref-11" aria-label="Reference 11: TheAgentCompany: Benchmarking LLM Agents on Consequential Real World Tasks">[11]</a> Xu et al. | NeurIPS 2025 | Figure 2 | Tested model versions in the paper</div></figure>')
    text('For example, the tested systems do worse on ownCloud than on Plane. Office tasks can be hard because an agent must use the interface and gather the right information.')
    text('Table 1 in the paper also reports 30.3% full success and a 39.3% score with partial credit for OpenHands with Gemini 2.5 Pro. The second number counts intermediate progress too.')
    text('Read each score together with its rule. Credit for finding a file and credit for completing the requested analysis answer different questions.')

    text('## 42 · Four Checks for a Score')
    text('**Before using an agent score, trace it back to the work.**')
    text('- What task did the agent receive, and what counted as success?')
    text('- How often did it succeed, and how much did each run cost?')
    text('- Who graded the result, and how was that grader checked?')
    text('- What tasks were tested, and which claims do they support?')
    text('The papers give us different tools for answering these questions. Use the ones that match the agent you are building.')

    text('## 43 · Thank you')
    text('Use the reference cards beside each paper to check the original figures and evaluation settings.')
